  "narrative": "# Tryptophan Metabolism Dysregulation: Key Findings and Biological Significance\n\n## Pathway Analysis\n\nThe metabolomics data reveals a striking enrichment of the **Tryptophan metabolism pathway (KEGG map00380)**, with an FDR of 0.0 and a fold enrichment of 625.475 — indicating that this pathway is profoundly altered in the treatment group. Five of the eight differentially abundant metabolites map directly to this pathway: L-Tryptophan (C00078), Serotonin (C00780), Quinolinic acid (C03722), 3-Hydroxyanthranilic acid (C00632), and Melatonin (C01598). Additionally, the \"Tryptophan catabolism leading to NAD+ production\" pathway (Wikipathways WP4210) shows even higher fold enrichment (1,174.8), suggesting that both the neuroactive and cofactor arms of tryptophan catabolism are impacted.\n\n## Key Driver Compounds\n\n**L-Tryptophan (C00078)** serves as the upstream entry point and primary driver of the observed changes. The KEGG reaction graph confirms a two-step conversion from L-Tryptophan → 5-Hydroxytryptophan → **Serotonin (C00780)**, establishing a verified upstream relationship. Serotonin, in turn, is converted to **Melatonin (C01598)** via a forward two-step path (Serotonin → N-Acetylserotonin → Melatonin), implicating the serotonin-melatonin axis in the treatment effect. **Quinolinic acid (C03722)** anchors the kynurenine branch and is directly annotated in both tryptophan metabolism and nicotinamide metabolism, linking the findings to NAD⁺ biosynthesis dysregulation.\n\n## Biological Significance\n\nDifferential regulation across these interconnected branches suggests that the treatment modulates tryptophan flux through both the neuroactive (serotonin/melatonin) and catabolic (NAD⁺/kynurenine) arms. Elevated or depleted tryptophan-derived metabolites can influence neuroinflammation, oxidative stress, and mitochondrial function, making this pathway relevant to neurological, metabolic, and immune-mediated conditions.",
